    {"claim_id":"jls-equality-kinds","text":"JLS 21 defines numerical, boolean and reference equality operators; reference equality is true only when both operands are null or refer to the same object/array, and equality comparison involving NaN is false.","source_ids":["jls21-equality"],"answer_locations":["核心结论","1 分钟版","3 分钟版","关键细节","原理机制","常见追问","易错点"]},
    {"claim_id":"object-equals-contract","text":"Java SE 21 Object.equals defaults to the most discriminating equivalence relation (for Object, same object), may be overridden, and its contract includes reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity, consistency and false for null.","source_ids":["java-se21-object"],"answer_locations":["核心结论","1 分钟版","3 分钟版","关键细节","原理机制","易错点"]},
    {"claim_id":"equals-hashcode-contract","text":"Java SE 21 Object specifies that equal objects must have equal hash codes, which is required for hash-based collections' general contract.","source_ids":["java-se21-object"],"answer_locations":["3 分钟版","原理机制","常见追问","易错点"]}
    ,{"claim_id":"string-and-objects-equals","text":"Java SE 21 documents String.equals as comparing a String to a specified object and Objects.equals as comparing two objects for equality with null-safe behavior.","source_ids":["java-se21-string","java-se21-objects"],"answer_locations":["1 分钟版","3 分钟版","关键细节","常见追问"]}
